Meh. A necessary conclusion to "Trickster's Choice," and I continued to enjoy a few of the characters, like Dove and Nawat. However, the plot was far too much for this author, who may be many things but is Not a master of military fiction. :} 
 My general impression that Aly was far too good at her job and that her allies, most especially the Raka ones, deferred to her far too easily (god or no god's blessing) remained. Not to mention the vast number of other players brought into the conspiracy. It simply wasn't believable that Someone wasn't going to screw up, get themselves killed, and loose (at a minimum) several cells worth of their friends. 
 Yeah, it's YA fiction, but still...

** spoiler alert ** 
 Setting: Half Moon Hollows, Kentucky: route 161; River Oaks (home), Cheney's Airstream trailer, Gabriel's home, Missy's condo; 
 Theme: dramatic life changing events; adjustment; covetousness (both by realtor and sister); 
 Characters: 
 Jane Jameson: hometown girl; children's librarian; inherited aunt's home; is laid off (head librarian has hated her since 3rd grade - and wants to hire her niece); goes out on a bender, meets Gabriel who is fascinated with her running mouth - she switches to coffee); heads home at 2 am, car stops, she gets out and falls into ditch, on hands and knees to climb out and drunk Bud, redneck (who left bar at same time) thinks she is a deer, takes out his riffle ad shoots her; Gabriel had followed, and he makes her a vampire to save her life; rest of story is her adjusting, while someone is out to get her (no real); 
 Gabriel Nightengale: vampire; pre-civil war; southern gentleman; rich; thinks he has found his bloodmate in Jane; he wants to do good by her change; takes care of Bud (knocks a tree on him when he is drunk, killing him) displeasing Jane when she finds out; they have sex once - and mine repeatedly comes out; jealous of others; 
 Zeb Lavelle: her best friend forever; comes over when she's been out of touch for 3 days (sleeping after vampire conversion), and she is overcome with bloodlust, and tries to bite him; he joins a vampire family support group to get a handle on how to deal with vampire Jane - and he does good; meets a were at the support group, and they ask Jane to be her maid of honor; 
 Aunt Lettie: always had an affinity with Jane; when she dies, she leaves her 'antique' house River Oaks to Jane (not older sister, with a family); she is haunting the house, and now that jane is a vamp, she can see her; positive support to Jane, but a bit wacky; 
 Jolene: werewolf; best friend a vamp (dusted now), family not supportive of her feelings, so she joined a support group and meets Zeb; she eats a lot (a lot); she is bubbly and outgoing, and takes Jane in stride; 
 Missy Houston: chairs new vampire welcome wagon; real estate; approached Lettie to sale; approaches Jane to sell; both said no; outgoing; at end we learn she purchased all the property around River Oaks and wants River Oaks for a condo development so she is doing things to get Jane to sell - up to and including getting Jenny (sister) on her side, to legally claim home and then sell to her and the to kidnapping Jane's family with intention of killing them all and claiming house; 
 Andrea Byrne: blood donor; enjoys the sexual high she gets when blood being taken; hired by Gabriel to ease jane into first time of taking blood from human; Jane reaches out to after as a new friend who can help her navigate the new vamp world; 
 Richard Cheney: vampire; friend of Gabriel's from childhood; did not give sanctuary to Gabriel when family kicked him out, and their friendship deteriorated; ladies' man; he and Jane banter back and forth; 
 Gilbert Wainwright: older male; always fascinated with supernatural, and now has bookstore and online book sales of the supernatural; store a mess; hires Jane with a plan to clean it all up; 
 Mrs. Stubblefield: head librarian; has it out for Jane since she was a child; after fires Jane, the library severely hurt by the loss of her, in the behavior of the children, in the books, etc.; at end offers Jane her job back... and Jane figures out she doesn't need them anymore... ahh 
 Sister Jenny/mother/father: not a lot of love lost between sisters - Jenny really figures she deserves the house; mother runs rough shod over Jane - wanting her to want what mother thinks is best; dad a history professor, and he is on Jane's side; 
 Summary: 
 Jane must adjust... humorous situations... goes out to a vampire bar with Andrea, and when a vamp is shaking the bartender up side down for the night's cash, Jane intervenes, gets the worse side of the fight (though she does get a few licks in), and goes home... He is later found dead, and Jane is blamed... someone is spreading rumors about her and Richard in inappropriate places (Gabriel jealous at first til finds out they are lies)... someone put anti freeze in dogs' water dish - her dog is most important to her... she gets some phone calls. When accused of Richard's death (his RV blown up), Missy challenges her to the death - if Jane wins, her innocence is proven, and she gets Missy's assets... but if Missy wins, she gets Jane's assets (including the house)... when Richard shows up (he had a fireproof area under the RV) so jane is innocent - Missy kidnaps her family... but Richard, Gabriel and Zeb come to the rescue... Missy and Jane have their fight to the death... and Zeb finds the encouragement to push Jane over the aggressive edge by yelling out that Missy tried to hurt her dog... and Jane wins. 
 Jane finds her feet - her family knows, but need time to adjust; Gabriel is giving her room, but tells her she is his bloodmate, and fascinates him, she has the perfect job in the paranormal bookstore that needs her, Zeb is getting married (and she's okay with it), 
 Memorable scenes: 
 Jane's internal monologue as she processes her best friend has a significant other, someone she has to share him with, another change for her to process; 
 Lettie has been in the house with Jane, and doing things like moving car keys and jackets - Jane finds out when she can see her now a vamp. 
 Mom and Jane have a fight over Jane not taking a job with Mom at a quilt shop... and Jane reflects that mom will ignore her for a week waiting for her to apologize, when Jane doesn't apologize, she just shows up as if there was never a fight. 
 Jane tells Gabriel his father will like him because he's excited to meet an actual witness to the civil war and since.

If I could, I'd give this a zero. There's absolutely no excuse for this book being published as is. What this needed was a good editor. Someone to cut out all the frivolous descriptions of commonplace events. One more lengthy discussion of tea time or the art of drinking wine and I think I would have gouged my eyes out! Add to that the enchanted love story between the temperamental, slow witted vampire and the damsel in distress witch and you have all the elements of an absolute bore. Nothing of any consequence happened in this book, nothing! 
 As a reader, I was bored; 
 As a female, I was disgusted with the prickly witch who one minute is all Miss independent but can not do anything to defend herself and must run to her love EVERYTIME for protection (she must have spent half the book being rescued by Matthew, the other half was of course spent drinking tea and wine); 
 As a scientist, this just grates on the nerves! There are pages which go into disorganized, excruciating details on alchemy and genetics. Most of these add no value to the story, they seem to be a haphazard collection of scientific moments. 
 What I was left with after finishing the book was that the author has visited Europe (or at least has read books), has some knowledge of history of science and wants to convince the world of these two facts. In that case, she succeeded. I'd argue that there are plenty of less painful ways of getting this point across though!
